I like to make a few comments about named weapons.  It seems to me that 
weapons that deal with specific types of creatures are pretty useless
(e.g. Dragonbane, Giantslayer, Ogresmasher, etc.).  Unlike many other
weapons which do extra damage against a vast majority of monsters, these
are very limited in power.  I once tested my Dragonslayer against a black
dragon, and it didn't seem to do any more damage than my Mjollnir, a little
less, in fact, since my Mjollnir was better enchanted at the time.  You
could argue that Mjollnir wouldn't do as well against a blue dragon, but
you know what I am saying.  My point is that, there should be some incentive
for using these weapons.  Sure, it's only right that some weapons should be
more powerful than others; that's what makes the game interesting.  But that
shouldn't be absolute.  There should be a 'kick' to every weapon. You should
be able to kill, say a green dragon, with Dragonbane in two or three hits, as
opposed to four, five, or even six hits by Mjollnir.  You should even have
a chance to instantly kill the dragon with it (kind of like Vorpal Blade).
This wouldn't unbalance the game, since everyone knows it's no small hassle
to carry too many weapons around.  But it sure would make me reconsider
tossing Giantslayer in a safe corner as soon as it shows up.

In one game I actually had 11 artifacts.  My god granted me Mjollnir, Vorpal
Blade, Magicbane, Dragonbane, Frost Brand, Fire Brand, and Giantslayer.  I 
found Cleaver after killing a Troll, Sunsword from an Aleax, and Demonbane 
from an Archon.  Finally I obtained Excalibur by dipping after converting to
Lawful.  I kept only Mjollnir and Frost Brand (as a backup).  Everything
else seemed more trouble than it was worth.

About Grayswandir,  I think that it was created rather carelessly.  Correct me
if I am wrong, but it appears that Grayswandir is a combination of Demonbane
and Sunsword.  They are all of the same alignment, so why carry Demonbane
around if you could have the more versatile Grayswandir (if you play the game
right, you can wish for Grayswandir every game, but you already know that)?

One last comment, and this is about Mjollnir.  It seems funny that the weapon
of the thunder god does not grant the wielder shock resistance.  After all,
Frost Brand, Fire Brand, Stormbringer, and (I think) Magicbane do similar 
things.  I know, Mjollnir is powerful enough as it is, but shock resistance 
isn't too difficult to get anyway.


